A YAML file is a text file used to store configuration settings and instructions in a structured, human-readable format.
YAML usage
GitHub Actions - Define automated workflows
Docker Compose - Define and configure multiple containers
Kubernetes - Define deployments, services and other resources
Ansible - Define automation and configuration tasks

Important YAML syntax rules
- Indentation matters. Use spaces, not tabs.
- A colon : separates a key from its value.
- A dash - represents an item in a list.
- # introduces a comment.
- YAML files commonly use .yaml or .yml extensions.
e.g 
services: 
  webster: #name of the service, it can be anything
    image: devopsdockeruh/simple-web-service  #ready-made image that will be pulled
    build: . #usually its either build or image but if both docker uses the local dockerfile in . instead of pulling from the internet
    volumes: 
    - ./log.txt:/usr/src/app/text.log 
    container_name: logger #name of the container
#webster should be indented to services, image, build, volumes should be indented to webster. 

FOR YAML FILES: The Four Automatic Default Filenames
When you run docker compose up without specifying a file, Docker automatically looks in your current directory for one of four filenames in this exact order of preference:
compose.yaml (Current official preferred standard), compose.yml, docker-compose.yaml (Legacy name, kept for backwards compatibility),  
docker-compose.yml (Legacy name, kept for backwards compatibility)  # Note: .yaml and .yml are the exact same file format; 
.yaml is just the official extension recommended by the YAML specification.)If both compose.yaml and docker-compose.yaml exist in the same folder,
Docker will prioritize compose.yaml.

- You can name your file anything you want—such as compose.dev.yaml, compose.prod.yaml, or my-app.yaml. Because Docker won't detect custom names automatically,
you simply pass the -f (--file) flag when running commands:   *docker compose -f compose.prod.yaml up -d

BUILD
build can be written in two ways: short syntax (a simple string) or long syntax (a detailed object with sub-options).
- Short Syntax, When passed as a single string, Docker treats that value as the build context (the folder containing your code)
and looks for a file named Dockerfile at the root of that folder:   
YAML
services:
  frontend:
    build: .  # Path to directory (can also be ./frontend or a Git URL)

- Long Syntax (All Sub-Options)
When you need more control—such as custom Dockerfile names, build-time variables, or multi-stage targets—you expand build into an object:
services:
  frontend:
    image: my-react-app:1.0
    build:
      # 1. Context & Dockerfile Location
      context: ./frontend
      dockerfile: Dockerfile.dev
      dockerfile_inline: |
        FROM node:20-alpine
        CMD ["echo", "Inline Dockerfile instead of a file"]

      # 2. Build-Time Variables & Secrets
      args:
        NODE_ENV: production
        API_URL: https://api.example.com
      secrets:
        - npm_token
      ssh:
        - default

      # 3. Multi-Stage Builds & Platforms
      target: development
      platforms:
        - linux/amd64
        - linux/arm64

      # 4. Caching & Rebuild Behavior
      cache_from:
        - my-react-app:cache
      cache_to:
        - type=inline
      no_cache: false
      pull: true

      # 5. Networking, Metadata & System Limits
      network: host
      extra_hosts:
        - "local.api:127.0.0.1"
      labels:
        com.example.description: "React Frontend"
      shm_size: '2gb'
      ulimits:
        nofile:
          soft: 65536
          hard: 65536
      isolation: default
      tags:
        - my-react-app:latest

Breakdown of Every build Option
Core Paths & Files:
- context (Required in long syntax): Path to the directory (relative to the Compose file) or a Git repository URL whose contents are sent to 
the Docker daemon during the build.
- dockerfile: Alternate path or filename for the Dockerfile, resolved relative to context (e.g., Dockerfile.prod).  
- dockerfile_inline: Lets you write raw Dockerfile instructions directly inside your compose.yaml instead of creating a separate file on disk. 
Cannot be used together with dockerfile.

Variables, Secrets & Authentication
- args: Key-value pairs (or a list like KEY=VALUE) passed to ARG instructions inside your Dockerfile. Unlike environment (which sets runtime variables), 
args exist only during the image build.   
- secrets: Grants the build process secure access to sensitive files or environment variables (defined in a top-level secrets: block) without baking them into 
the final image layers.
- ssh: Passes SSH agent sockets or keys into the builder so RUN commands can clone private Git repositories securely.

Multi-Stage & Multi-Architecture
- target: Stops the build at a specific named stage (FROM ... AS <stage_name>) in a multi-stage Dockerfile. 
Very useful for sharing one Dockerfile between a development target and a production target.   
- platforms: List of target OS/CPU architectures (e.g., linux/amd64, linux/arm64) to cross-compile for using Buildx.

Cache & Pull Control
- cache_from / cache_to: External images or registry locations used to import/export layer caches—commonly used to speed up builds in CI/CD pipelines.
- no_cache: Boolean (true/false). Forces Docker to build every layer from scratch without reusing local cache.
- pull: Boolean (true/false). Forces Docker to always download the newest version of base images (FROM ...) before building.

Network, Metadata & Resource Limits
- network: Sets the network mode for RUN instructions during the build (e.g., host, none, or a custom network).
- extra_hosts: Adds custom hostname-to-IP mappings to /etc/hosts inside the build container.
- labels / tags: Attaches OCI metadata labels or additional image tags to the resulting image.  
- shm_size: Sets the size of /dev/shm (shared memory) during the build—helpful if headless browsers or compilers run out of memory during builds.
- ulimits: Overrides default container resource limits (like max open files nofile) during the build.
- additional_contexts: Maps named extra directories or images so your Dockerfile can copy files from outside the primary context folder (COPY --from=shared_lib ...).

Important CLI Behavior to Remember
Compose only builds an image automatically if it doesn't exist locally yet. If you edit your Dockerfile or source code later, 
running docker compose up alone will reuse the old image. To apply changes, run: 
'*docker compose up --build (rebuilds images and restarts containers)  
'*docker compose build (rebuilds images without starting containers)  

Using a ready image with Docker Compose
Using a ready made image means build is skipped, the yaml file will be like:
services:
  nginx:
    image: nginx:1.29
  database:
    image: postgres:18 
#Both of the containers can now be started with command docker compose up
